Brine-Jack
Ingredients: TECH HOUSE + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Risers + 1970s ERA
Creative Catalyst: Reverse-engineered from a salt-encrusted, heat-warped cassette tape unearthed from a 1977 municipal harbor time capsule.

Brine-Jack grafts the rolling, sub-heavy four-on-the-floor propulsion of modern tech house onto the eerie, tape-flanged dissonance of 1970s BBC Radiophonic scores and maritime public warning broadcasts. Crisp, swung 909 hi-hats clatter against damp spring-reverb acoustics, while menacing analog synthesizers and tape-decay risers slowly ramp tension to a boiling point, dropping not into euphoric party releases, but into hollow, sea-drowned vacuums inhabited by phantom voices.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 125–128 BPM; swung tech-house kick engine layered over syncopated 1970s studio congas, underpinned by an elastic, pendulum-style rolling sub-bass that leans heavy into the off-beats.
- Harmonic Palette: Phrygian and Locrian modal progressions, microtonal drift caused by wow-and-flutter tape effects, eerie minor second clusters, and detuned Mellotron choirs drenched in vintage EMT 140 plate reverb.
- Production Signatures:
  1. The Capstan Riser: Tension builds generated not by digital white-noise sweeps, but by accelerating reel-to-reel tape motor feedback, modular siren sweeps, and squealing spring-tank overloads that climb across 16 bars.
  2. Corrupted Head-Bleed: Deliberate, subtle audio ghosts—reversed fragments of the upcoming bassline or spoken word drifting into the left-right stereo margins beats before they actually trigger, mimicking magnetic print-through on aged tape.
  3. Bucket-Brigade Flanging: Drastic analog jet-flanging applied to tech-house open hats and rimshots, creating a swooshing, wet texture reminiscent of wind whipping off a harbor wall.
- Dynamics: Extended, claustrophobic ascents punctuated by sudden dropouts where the kick drum vanishes entirely, leaving nothing but an icy sub-drone, cold mechanical clicks, and a solitary spectral vocal phrase before slamming back into the heavy groove.

Scene & Origin Lore
Brine-Jack sparked along the salt-slicked docklands of Kingston upon Hull and Grimsby in the late 2010s, after salvage workers unsealed a rusted 1977 municipal time capsule buried in the foundations of Victoria Pier. Inside rested a severely warped cassette tape labeled "Harbor Signals & Folk Rehearsals," its magnetic layer half-eaten by brine. Underground producers digitized the decayed audio, isolating its uncanny tape swells, minor-key Moog drones, and chilling maritime choir rehearsals, layering them beneath relentless, club-ready rolling tech-house basslines. The resulting subculture gathers in derelict tidal slipways, decommissioned cold-storage warehouses, and rusty trawler hulls. Attired in heavy oiled-wool fishermen's ganseys, yellow foul-weather slickers, and industrial ear protection, partygoers dance with a stiff, side-to-side trance sway dubbed the "listing step," while single rotating amber hazard beacons cut through heavy, cold fog machine smoke.

Typical Instruments Used: EMS VCS3 "Putney" synthesizer (for rising wind-squalls and modular alarms), Mellotron M400 (corrupted choir and string tapes), Roland TR-909 and TR-808 (layered with 1970s acoustic Ludwig kick thuds), Roland RE-201 Space Echo, Moog Minimoog (for sinister sub-chugs), Revox B77 reel-to-reel tape decks (for live tape manipulation and risers).

For Fans Of: The Caretaker, Ricardo Villalobos, Demdike Stare, Jamie Jones (at an abandoned lighthouse at 4 AM).

