Track 2: Who Stood the Dogwatch?
- Story & Context: Confined to the fog-swept glass lantern room of Spurn Point after a bitter dispute with the coastal board, relief watchman Eamonn Vance spent forty-eight hours obsessively dubbing side B of the time-capsule tape over an unrelenting Roland 909 groove. Surrounded by cold chicory coffee grounds and the suffocating odor of damp, salt-stiff wool, Vance recognized the distorted vocal hums on the tape as the youth choir lost aboard an unregistered harbor tender during the 1978 gales—a casualty list long erased from official ledgers. He fed his own tear-choked radio confession through a battered bucket-brigade flanger, laying down a manic, rolling house bassline punctuated by his hoarse cry into the ship-to-shore receiver: "I saw your mast-head flare go out across the spit."
